WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.180
In this video we will set up Submilli with
a coding agent,

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:06.980
build a billing package, create its
blueprint,

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:11.140
and run a program. Start with the Submilli
setup prompt for Claude

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:14.980
Code or Codex. Ask your assistant to
follow it:

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:19.540
read the skill instructions, install the C
L I and Submilli server,

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:21.860
and set up the skill in your project.

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:24.980
Then ask it to use the skill for this
quickstart.

00:00:26.267 --> 00:00:29.947
Ask your coding agent to create the
billing package using the quickstart

00:00:30.027 --> 00:00:34.187
example. The skill guides it through the
scaffold and implementation.

00:00:34.747 --> 00:00:39.147
Our example exposes one function: list
charges for a customer.

00:00:39.627 --> 00:00:43.547
Its capability declares the operation and
customer ID;

00:00:43.587 --> 00:00:47.867
the check call asks the runtime for
permission before reading any charges.

00:00:48.347 --> 00:00:52.347
In your project terminal, run the build
check and publish-local commands

00:00:52.427 --> 00:00:56.187
shown here to build the package and
install it in the local package

00:00:56.267 --> 00:00:56.907
store.

00:00:58.167 --> 00:01:01.767
Ask your coding agent to create the
blueprint for that package,

00:01:01.847 --> 00:01:06.087
then review its rules. It lists the
billing package and requires

00:01:06.167 --> 00:01:08.527
a customer ID from your application.

00:01:08.727 --> 00:01:13.207
Allow charge lookups only when the
requested ID matches that value.

00:01:13.447 --> 00:01:16.327
Everything else is denied. Finally,

00:01:16.407 --> 00:01:19.927
run the blueprint lint command in your
project terminal to check those

00:01:20.007 --> 00:01:22.807
rules against the package’s capability
schema.

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:28.180
Generate a random token with Open S S L
and export it in your

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:32.500
terminal. This is the shared secret your
application will use to call

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:35.620
the Submilli server. In that same
terminal,

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:39.460
start the Submilli server and register the
blueprint using the commands

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:43.700
shown here. Ask your coding agent to
create the example application

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:47.620
and program from the quickstart. The
application reads the exported

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:51.700
token and binds Customer A’s ID. Run the
program with

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:56.100
the node command shown here, and the
permitted charge lookup succeeds.

00:01:57.367 --> 00:02:01.287
Ask your coding agent to create the
quickstart’s second program,

00:02:01.367 --> 00:02:05.527
then run it from your terminal. It adds
the returned charges and prints

00:02:05.607 --> 00:02:08.887
the calculated total, then asks for
Customer B.

00:02:09.367 --> 00:02:11.847
Before the package reads protected data,

00:02:11.967 --> 00:02:15.767
its check receives that argument, the
blueprint filter fails,

00:02:15.807 --> 00:02:18.727
and the Submilli server returns a
permission error.

00:02:18.967 --> 00:02:22.647
The first call completed; the
cross-customer call did not.

00:02:22.887 --> 00:02:25.527
Your application binds the request
context,

00:02:25.567 --> 00:02:29.367
and the runtime enforces the rule around
the code the agent wrote.
